---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ToolShell from '../components/ToolShell.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Layout title="Convert SRT to WebVTT: make subtitles for HTML5 video" description="Convert SRT, ASS, SSA, SAMI, MicroDVD or MPL2 subtitles to WebVTT for HTML5 video in your browser. Check timestamp and style changes before downloading.">
  <h1>Convert to WebVTT</h1>
  <p class="lede">Convert SRT to WebVTT for an HTML5 <code>&lt;track&gt;</code>, a web video player, or a caption upload that requires <code>.vtt</code>.</p>

  <ToolShell columns={['#', 'Start', 'End', 'Text']} />

  <section class="help">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>Upload the subtitle file, review the cue preview, and download a UTF-8 WebVTT file. The converter adds the required <code>WEBVTT</code> header.</p>
    <h2>Supported input formats</h2>
    <p>Convert SRT, ASS, SSA, SAMI, MicroDVD, or MPL2. SRT is the common choice for web captions. MicroDVD uses video frames, so select the video's frame rate when the file does not include one.</p>
    <h2>SRT and WebVTT timestamps</h2>
    <p>SRT uses a comma for milliseconds. WebVTT uses a period. The converter changes the separator and keeps the cue start and end times.</p>
    <pre><code>SRT:  00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:14,800
VTT:  00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.800</code></pre>
    <h2>What happens to styles?</h2>
    <p>Inline bold or italic tags can be kept, along with underlining. ASS positions, alignment, karaoke timing, and other ASS-only styles are reported and removed because WebVTT cannot represent them the same way. Review the loss report before publishing captions.</p>
    <h2>Common errors</h2>
    <p>If a browser rejects the file, check that the download has a <code>.vtt</code> extension and starts with <code>WEBVTT</code>. If cue times look wrong, the input may use the wrong frame rate or encoding. <a href="/subtitle-validator">Run the WebVTT validator</a> to find header errors and timestamp, overlap, or empty-cue problems.</p>
  </section>

  <Faq items={[
    { question: 'Can I convert SRT to WebVTT?', answer: 'Yes. Upload the SRT file and download a WebVTT file with the required WEBVTT header.' },
    { question: 'Why do WebVTT times use dots?', answer: 'SRT uses a comma before milliseconds. WebVTT uses a period. The converter changes this for you.' },
    { question: 'Will ASS styles work in WebVTT?', answer: 'Some inline bold or italic tags can stay. ASS positions, karaoke timing, and other ASS-only styles are removed and listed in the report.' },
  ]} />

  <RelatedTools tools={[
    { href: '/convert-to-srt', label: 'Convert back to SRT', description: 'Make an SRT for players and editors that do not accept WebVTT.' },
    { href: '/subtitle-validator', label: 'Validate WebVTT', description: 'Check the header, timestamps, order, and cue text.' },
    { href: '/subtitle-shifter', label: 'Fix subtitle timing', description: 'Move every cue earlier or later by one offset.' },
    { href: '/srt-cleaner', label: 'Clean the captions', description: 'Remove tags and SDH notes before publishing.' },
  ]} />
</Layout>

<script>
  import { mountConverter } from '../components/converter.ts';
  mountConverter('vtt');
</script>

<style>
  :global(.tool td:nth-child(-n + 3)) { white-space: nowrap; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  :global(.tool td:last-child) { width: 100%; }
</style>
